  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency response for internally displaced persons and affected populations impacted by tropical cyclones in the PhilippinesEmergency response for internally displaced persons and affected populations impacted by tropical cyclones in the Philippines</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since July 2025, the Philippines has been affected by a combination of tropical storms, cyclones, and earthquakes. Most recently, on 3 November, Typhoon Kalmaegi (Tino in the Philippines) entered the Philippine Area of Responsibility, and made landfall in the Visayas regions and Southwestern Tagalog Region, bringing torrential rains and causing heavy floods. In addition, Typhoon Uwan (international name Fung-Wong) is rapidly intensifying and expecting to enter the Philippine Area of Responsibility by late 7 November or early 8 November, potentially as a super typhoon; Uwan will also bring deteriorated weather conditions in several regions of the country, potentially compounding the adverse impact of Typhoon Kalmaegi and the various disasters from earlier in the year.IOM aims to mitigate the adverse effects of tropical cyclones, particularly Kalmaegi and Uwan, as well as the compounded impacts of additional disasters in the target areas, on the most vulnerable, affected and/or displaced communities in the Philippines. This is being achieved through coordinating with local authorities to respond to urgent needs for Camp Coordination and Camp Management (CCCM), water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH), emergency shelter, and non-food item (NFI) distribution, to enable affected populations to access safe living conditions and life-saving assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
